                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2524'                         EXADDR:
  2524'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2525'   32 2B7F'              	STA	SRNGF
  2528'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2529'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  252B'   CA 2554'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  252E'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  252F'   CD 2569'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2532'   DA 2552'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2535'   22 2B80'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2538'   22 2B82'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  253B'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  253C'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  253E'   C2 254B'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2541'   13                    	INX	D
  2542'   CD 2569'              	CALL	EXNUM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  2545'   DA 2552'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2548'   22 2B82'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  254B'                         EXA_YS:
  254B'   EB                    	XCHG
  254C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  254E'   32 2B7F'              	STA	SRNGF
  2551'   C9                    	RET
  2552'                         EXA_NO:
  2552'   EB                    	XCHG
  2553'   C9                    	RET
  2554'                         EXA_AL:
  2554'   23                    	INX	H
  2555'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2556'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2559'   22 2B80'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  255C'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  255F'   22 2B82'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2562'   E1                    	POP	H
  2563'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2565'   32 2B7F'              	STA	SRNGF
  2568'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  2569'                         EXNUM:
  2569'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  256A'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  256C'   D8                    	RC
  256D'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  256F'   3F                    	CMC
  2570'   D8                    	RC
  2571'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2574'                         EXN_LP:
  2574'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2575'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2577'   DA 2591'              	JC	EXN_DN
  257A'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  257C'   D2 2591'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  257F'   13                    	INX	D
  2580'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2581'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2582'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2584'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2585'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2586'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2587'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2588'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2589'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  258A'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  258B'   D1                    	POP	D
  258C'   09                    	DAD	B
  258D'   C1                    	POP	B
  258E'   C3 2574'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  2591'                         EXN_DN:
  2591'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  2592'   C9                    	RET
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  2593'                         EX_S:
  2593'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2594'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2595'   CA 25D7'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  2598'   32 2B84'              	STA	SDELIM
  259B'   23                    	INX	H
  259C'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  259F'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  25A2'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  25A3'   CD 25DD'              	CALL	SCOPY
  25A6'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  25A7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25A8'   CA 25D7'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  25AB'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  25AE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25B0'   32 2B4C'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  25B3'   11 2B96'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  25B6'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  25B8'   CD 25DD'              	CALL	SCOPY
  25BB'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  25BC'   32 2B95'              	STA	SREPLN
  25BF'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25C0'   32 2B85'              	STA	SGFLAG
  25C3'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  25C4'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  25C6'   C2 25CF'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  25C9'   23                    	INX	H
  25CA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25CC'   32 2B85'              	STA	SGFLAG
  25CF'                         EX_SG:
  25CF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  25D0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25D1'   C2 236E'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  25D4'   C3 25FD'              	JMP	SUBGO
  25D7'                         SUBNOP:
  25D7'   21 2426'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  25DA'   C3 237C'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  25DD'                         SCOPY:
  25DD'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  25DF'                         SCP_LP:
  25DF'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  25E0'   B9                    	CMP	C
  25E1'   D2 25FA'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  25E4'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  25E5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25E6'   CA 25FA'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  25E9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  25EA'   21 2B84'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


  25ED'   BE                    	CMP	M
  25EE'   E1                    	POP	H
  25EF'   CA 25F9'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  25F2'   12                    	STAX	D
  25F3'   13                    	INX	D
  25F4'   23                    	INX	H
  25F5'   04                    	INR	B
  25F6'   C3 25DF'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  25F9'                         SCP_SK:
  25F9'   23                    	INX	H
  25FA'                         SCP_DN:
  25FA'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25FB'   12                    	STAX	D
  25FC'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  25FD'                         SUBGO:
  25FD'   CD 0FD9'              	CALL	GZONE
  2600'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2603'   22 2B93'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2606'   21 2B90'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2609'   CD 044D'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  260C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  260D'   32 2B86'              	STA	SANY
  2610'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2613'   22 2B88'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2616'   3A 2B7F'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2619'   B7                    	ORA	A
  261A'   CA 262C'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  261D'   CD 1EF8'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2620'   DA 26A8'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2623'   2A 2B80'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2626'   CD 0F47'              	CALL	GOLN
  2629'   C3 2632'              	JMP	SUBLP
  262C'                         SUB_1L:
  262C'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  262F'   22 2B8A'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2632'                         SUBLP:
  2632'   CD 26AE'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2635'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2636'   CA 2644'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2639'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  263B'   32 2B86'              	STA	SANY
  263E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2641'   22 2B88'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2644'                         SUB_NX:
  2644'   2A 2B8A'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2647'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2648'   22 2B8A'              	SHLD	SLINES
  264B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  264C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  264D'   CA 266B'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2650'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


  2653'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2654'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2657'   3A 2B86'              	LDA	SANY
  265A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  265B'   CA 2665'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  265E'   2A 2B88'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2661'   23                    	INX	H
  2662'   22 2B88'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2665'                         SUB_AE:
  2665'   CD 0A81'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2668'   D2 2632'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  266B'                         SUBDON:
  266B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  266E'   3A 2B86'              	LDA	SANY
  2671'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2672'   CA 2690'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2675'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2677'   32 2ACC'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  267A'   2A 2B88'              	LHLD	SBACK
  267D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  267E'   B5                    	ORA	L
  267F'   CA 268A'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2682'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2684'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2687'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  268A'                         SUB_PL:
  268A'   CD 0FF1'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  268D'   C3 07DF'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  2690'                         SUBNFD:
  2690'   21 2B90'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  2693'   CD 03F4'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2696'   2A 2B93'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2699'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  269C'   21 2406'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  269F'   CD 237C'              	CALL	EXERR
  26A2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  26A5'   C3 2377'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  26A8'                         SUBBAD:
  26A8'   21 23F4'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  26AB'   C3 2371'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  26AE'                         SUBLIN:
  26AE'   AF                    	XRA	A
  26AF'   32 2B87'              	STA	SLCHG
  26B2'   CD 272D'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  26B5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  26B8'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  26B8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  26BB'   DA 26C3'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  26BE'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  26C0'   C2 26B8'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  26C3'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  26C3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  26C6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  26C7'   CD 1593'              	CALL	CEND
  26CA'   D1                    	POP	D
  26CB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  26CE'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  26CF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  26D2'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  26D3'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  26D4'   FA 2726'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  26D7'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  26D8'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  26D9'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  26DC'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  26DF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  26E2'   DA 2726'              	JC	SUBL_D
  26E5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  26E8'   EB                    	XCHG
  26E9'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  26EC'   EB                    	XCHG
  26ED'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  26F0'   EB                    	XCHG
  26F1'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  26F2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  26F5'   D1                    	POP	D
  26F6'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  26F7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  26FA'   3A 2B86'              	LDA	SANY
  26FD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26FE'   C2 270B'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2701'   3A 2B87'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2704'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2705'   C2 270B'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2708'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  270B'                         SUBL_C:
  270B'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  270E'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  270F'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2711'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2714'   11 2B95'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2717'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  271A'                         SUBL_I:
  271A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  271C'   32 2B87'              	STA	SLCHG
  271F'   3A 2B85'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2722'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2723'   C2 26C3'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2726'                         SUBL_D:
  2726'   CD 272D'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2729'   3A 2B87'              	LDA	SLCHG
  272C'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  272D'                         SUBBOL:
  272D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2730'   CD 0648'              	CALL	BOL
  2733'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2736'                         VC_SRCH:
  2736'   CD 07BE'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2739'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  273A'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	GETCNT
  273D'   22 2B75'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2740'   C1                    	POP	B
  2741'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2742'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2744'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2746'   CA 274B'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2749'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  274B'                         VS_DIR:
  274B'   32 2B4A'              	STA	SDIR
  274E'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2751'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2754'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2755'   CD 1C94'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2758'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2759'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  275A'   3A 2B21'              	LDA	RLLEN
  275D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  275E'   CA 2773'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2761'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2762'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2764'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


  2767'   19                    	DAD	D
  2768'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  276A'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  276B'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  276E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2770'   32 2B4C'              	STA	SHAVE
  2773'                         VS_OLD:
  2773'   3A 2B4C'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2776'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2777'   CA 2783'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  277A'   3A 2B4A'              	LDA	SDIR
  277D'   32 2B4B'              	STA	SDIRE
  2780'   C3 27AC'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2783'                         VS_NOP:
  2783'   21 2426'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2786'   C3 237C'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2789'                         VC_N:
  2789'   CD 07BE'              	CALL	SETMOV
  278C'   3A 2B4A'              	LDA	SDIR
  278F'   C3 2799'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2792'                         VCB_N:
  2792'   CD 07BE'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2795'   3A 2B4A'              	LDA	SDIR
  2798'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2799'                         VN_GO:
  2799'   32 2B4B'              	STA	SDIRE
  279C'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	GETCNT
  279F'   22 2B75'              	SHLD	SCNT
  27A2'   3A 2B4C'              	LDA	SHAVE
  27A5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27A6'   CA 2783'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  27A9'   C3 27AC'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  27AC'                         SRCHN:
  27AC'   CD 0FD9'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  27AF'   CD 0718'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and a ^C may stop it
  27B2'                         SN_LP:
  27B2'   CD 27E7'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  27B5'   DA 27D2'              	JC	SN_BAD
  27B8'   2A 2B75'              	LHLD	SCNT
  27BB'   2B                    	DCX	H
  27BC'   22 2B75'              	SHLD	SCNT
  27BF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  27C0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  27C1'   C2 27B2'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  27C4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  27C7'   CD 074F'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  27CA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


  27CC'   32 2B57'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  27CF'   C3 0FF1'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  27D2'                         SN_BAD:
  27D2'   CD 074F'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  27D5'   DA 0767'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  27D8'   CD 075A'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  27DB'   21 2406'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  27DE'   CD 237C'              	CALL	EXERR
  27E1'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  27E4'   C3 2377'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  27E7'                         SRCHGO:
  27E7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27E9'   32 2B7E'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  27EC'   CD 2802'              	CALL	SRCHW
  27EF'   D0                    	RNC
  27F0'   CD 0753'              	CALL	BRKQ
  27F3'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  27F4'   3A 2B4B'              	LDA	SDIRE
  27F7'   2F                    	CMA
  27F8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  27FB'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27FC'   32 2B7E'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  27FF'   C3 2802'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2802'                         SRCHW:
  2802'   3A 2B4B'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2805'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2808'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  280B'   22 2AC9'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  280E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  280F'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2812'                         SW_LP:
  2812'   CD 0753'              	CALL	BRKQ		; a ^C spent the count, here or on the way to
  2815'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2816'   CD 2822'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2819'   D0                    	RNC
  281A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  281D'   D2 2812'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2820'   37                    	STC
  2821'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2822'                         SRCH1:
  2822'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2825'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2828'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2829'   FA 284D'              	JM	SR1_BK
  282C'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  282F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2832'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2835'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2836'   3A 2B7E'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2839'   B7                    	ORA	A
  283A'   CA 2843'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  283D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  283E'   B1                    	ORA	C
  283F'   37                    	STC
  2840'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2841'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2842'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2843'                         SR1_FW:
  2843'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2846'   D8                    	RC
  2847'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  284A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  284D'                         SR1_BK:
  284D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2850'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2853'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2856'   D8                    	RC
  2857'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  285A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  285D'   D8                    	RC
  285E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2861'                         MSGNAM:
  2861'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2862'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2864'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2867'   E1                    	POP	H
  2868'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2869'   23                    	INX	H
  286A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  286B'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  286D'   C2 287A'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2870'   21 2494'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2873'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2876'   E1                    	POP	H
  2877'   C3 28C4'              	JMP	MN_END
  287A'                         MN_HAS:
  287A'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  287C'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  287F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2880'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2881'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2882'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2883'   CA 2891'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2886'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2887'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2889'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  288C'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  288E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2891'                         MN_NAM:
  2891'   E1                    	POP	H
  2892'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2893'   23                    	INX	H
  2894'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2896'                         MN_NM:
  2896'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2897'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2899'   CA 28A4'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  289C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  289F'   23                    	INX	H
  28A0'   0D                    	DCR	C
  28A1'   C2 2896'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  28A4'                         MN_EXT:
  28A4'   E1                    	POP	H
  28A5'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  28A8'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  28A9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28AA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  28AC'   CA 28C4'              	JZ	MN_END
  28AF'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  28B1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28B4'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  28B6'                         MN_XL:
  28B6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28B7'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  28B9'   CA 28C4'              	JZ	MN_END
  28BC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28BF'   23                    	INX	H
  28C0'   0D                    	DCR	C
  28C1'   C2 28B6'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  28C4'                         MN_END:
  28C4'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  28C6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  28C9'                         MSGFLG:
  28C9'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  28CB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28CC'   CA 28DC'              	JZ	MF_RO
  28CF'   3A 2ACC'              	LDA	MODF
  28D2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28D3'   CA 28DC'              	JZ	MF_RO
  28D6'   21 249E'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  28D9'   CD 28F1'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  28DC'                         MF_RO:
  28DC'   3A 2B37'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  28DF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28E0'   CA 28E9'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  28E3'   21 24A9'              	LXI	H,SROM
  28E6'   CD 28F1'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  28E9'                         MF_NEW:
  28E9'   3A 2B35'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  28EC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28ED'   C8                    	RZ
  28EE'   21 24B4'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  28F1'                         MF_PUT:
  28F1'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  28F2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28F3'   C2 28FF'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  28F6'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  28F8'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28F9'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  28FB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28FE'   E1                    	POP	H
  28FF'                         MF_STR:
  28FF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2902'                         EMITW:
  2902'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2903'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2904'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2906'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2909'   CD 2927'              	CALL	EWDIG
  290C'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  290F'   CD 2927'              	CALL	EWDIG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


  2912'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2915'   CD 2927'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2918'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  291B'   CD 2927'              	CALL	EWDIG
  291E'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  291F'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2921'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2924'   D1                    	POP	D
  2925'   C1                    	POP	B
  2926'   C9                    	RET
  2927'                         EWDIG:
  2927'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2929'                         EW_LP:
  2929'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  292A'   93                    	SUB	E
  292B'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  292C'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  292D'   9A                    	SBB	D
  292E'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  292F'   DA 2936'              	JC	EW_DN
  2932'   0C                    	INR	C
  2933'   C3 2929'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2936'                         EW_DN:
  2936'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2937'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2938'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2939'   C2 293F'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  293C'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  293D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  293E'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  293F'                         EW_PR:
  293F'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2940'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2942'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2945'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2947'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2948'                         ISEMPT:
  2948'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  294B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  294C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  294D'   C0                    	RNZ
  294E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2951'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2952'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2953'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2954'                         MSGCTG:
  2954'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2957'   CD 2861'              	CALL	MSGNAM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  295A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  295C'   CD 28C9'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  295F'   CD 2948'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2962'   C2 296B'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2965'   21 24C7'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2968'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  296B'                         MG_POS:
  296B'   21 24BA'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  296E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2971'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2974'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2975'   CD 2902'              	CALL	EMITW
  2978'   21 24C1'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  297B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  297E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2981'   23                    	INX	H
  2982'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2983'   CD 2902'              	CALL	EMITW
  2986'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2989'   23                    	INX	H
  298A'   D1                    	POP	D
  298B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  298E'   C8                    	RZ
  298F'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2990'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2992'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2995'   E1                    	POP	H
  2996'   C3 2902'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2999'                         FILMSG:
  2999'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  299C'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  299E'   C8                    	RZ
  299F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  29A2'   CD 2861'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  29A5'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  29A6'   C3 28C9'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  29A9'                         WRMSG:
  29A9'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  29AC'   CD 2861'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  29AF'   21 24D9'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  29B2'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  29B5'                         MSGPST:
  29B5'   3A 2B34'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  29B8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29B9'   C8                    	RZ
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  29BA'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  29BB'   AF                    	XRA	A
  29BC'   32 2B34'              	STA	MSGWHT
  29BF'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  29C0'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  29C2'   CA 29DE'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  29C5'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  29C7'   CA 29E4'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  29CA'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  29CC'   CA 29D5'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  29CF'   CD 2954'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  29D2'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  29D5'                         MP_BIG:
  29D5'   21 2475'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  29D8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  29DB'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  29DE'                         MP_FIL:
  29DE'   CD 2999'              	CALL	FILMSG
  29E1'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  29E4'                         MP_WRT:
  29E4'   CD 29A9'              	CALL	WRMSG
  29E7'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  29EA'                         VC_CTLG:
  29EA'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  29EC'   32 2B34'              	STA	MSGWHT
  29EF'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  29F1'   32 2ACD'              	STA	RINTENT
  29F4'   C3 06EF'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  29F7'                         CMDTAB:
  29F7'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  29F8'   29EA'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  29FA'   68                    	DB	'h'
  29FB'   0817'                 	DW	VC_H
  29FD'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  29FE'   0841'                 	DW	VC_L
  2A00'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2A01'   0912'                 	DW	VC_J
  2A03'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2A04'   0924'                 	DW	VC_K
  2A06'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2A07'   0874'                 	DW	VC_0
  2A09'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2A0A'   0892'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2A0C'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2A0D'   08D0'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2A0F'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2A10'   08F4'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2A12'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2A13'   0B03'                 	DW	VC_W
  2A15'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2A16'   0B54'                 	DW	VC_B
